  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe introduce a novel approach based on non-integer sampling and mixed-integer convex optimization to address the minimum overlap problem. Unlike the previous spectral and gradient-based methods, we parametrize the optimal solution using a fundamental shift-parameter \u03bb, combining periodicity-based constraints with a continuous function space optimization. This hybrid strategy allows escaping local optima by leveraging the periodic structure of the solution space while maintaining feasibility.\n\nThe core innovation involves defining h(x) as saturations of sinusoidal functions:\nh(x) = tanh(\u03b2 \u00b7 sin(2\u03c0x) + a) + c  [adjusted to [0,1] range]\nallowing automatic satisfaction of the integral constraint. We optimize over the saturation points, amplitude, and frequency using a majorization technique that guarantees monotonic improvement of the upper bound. This formulation preserves the functional structure while enabling efficient local search via convex relaxations.\n\nThe method addresses the original problem with several key improvements:\n1. Reduced dimensionality by focusing on a single fundamental parameter\n2. Automatic satisfaction of integral constraint\n3. Guaranteed monotonic improvement during optimization\n4. Compatibility with multiple initial conditions\n5. Mathematical tractability through carefully constructed convex relaxations\n\nMathematical specification:\nDefine the objective as:\nC_5(\u03bb) = (1/\u03bb) * max_{k} \u222b[0,2] h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\nSubject to:\n0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n\u222b[0,2]h(x)dx = 1\n\nwhere h(x) is defined through its graph structure:\n\nh(x) = [f(x) + z] / [f(2) + z]   [normalization]\nwith f(x) = tanh(\u03b2 * sin(2\u03c0x/\u03bb) + a) + b\n\nParameters: \n- \u03b2: periodicity coefficient (large \u2192 high oscillation)\n- a,b: amplitude+position \n- z: saturation bias\n- \u03bb: sampling period\n\nConstraints are enforced through projected gradients in the optimization:\n\nGradient projection: For Lagrangian L(c) = smooth_max(c) + \u03bb\u00b7constraint(c)\nupdate = argmin cg(c + dc) s.t. ||dc||\u2264\u03b3\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n- Start with uniform grid of period \u03bb\u2080 \u2208 [0.5,4]\n- Use parent solution if available for warm start\n\n2. Feasibility projection:\ndef project_to_feasible(c):\n    c.clip(min_clip, max_clip)\n    scale = target_integral - current_integral\n    c += scale / (n_points * dx)\n    return c\n\n3. Smooth optimization loop:\nfor epoch in range(max_iters):\n    - Compute full correlation metric\n    - Apply gradient descent on current best \u03bb\n    - Perform spatial smoothing using Savitzky-Golay filter\n    - Project coefficients to satisfy constraints\n    - Adjust learning rate by backtracking line search\n\n4. Resolution adaptation:\ndef scale_resolution(old_n, target_n):\n    ratio = target_n / old_n\n    return old_n * ratio, decomposition_in_place\n\n5. Metric evaluation:\ndef compute_upper_bound(h):\n    corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")\n    return dx * max(corr)  [dx=2/n_points]\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Variation 1: Use tanh()/sigmoid basis vs logistic function\n- Variation 2: Simulated annealing vs gradient descent\n- Variation 3: Sequential \u03bb refinement vs global search\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\n- Dominant cost: \u2248 O(n_points * log(target_c5))\n- Total execution time: 1500s for n=1000+ points\n- Fallback mechanisms:\n  - If oscillations too high: revert to previous state\n  - If integral constraint violated: use constrained optimizer\n  - Early termination on stability of bound\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Verify computational constraints via forward simulation\n- Check integral of result matches target\n- Ensure all values are in [0,1]\u00b1tolerance\n- Final check: correlate(optimized_h, 1-optimized_h)\n\nAll operations will be performed via validated numpy implementations, with helper functions computed as:\n1. Basis function evaluation\n2. Projection mechanisms\n3. Metric computation\n4. Optimization bounds\n\nThe algorithm progresses by:\n1. Initial random search on period parameter\n2. Adaptive intensity refinement at promising periods\n3. Local optimization with constraints\n4. Final metric evaluation and cleanup",
